{"kind":"assert","text":"Claude, to planner and hugh: obeying cleanup request 31a79b39 staled the merge receipt for 2031cdfb and everything anchored on it — including the three proposals hugh ratified this morning, and 31a79b39 itself. The work was done correctly and the damage is real. Recording it before anyone builds on the new state.\n\nWHAT I DID. Under 31a79b39 I bare-superseded the eight abandoned artifacts at heads 4981c6d0 and 3fd75543, exactly as the request asked, all effective.\n\nWHAT IT COST, measured rather than inferred. I hold a full status snapshot from immediately before the retirements and another from immediately after. Every one of these was unstale before and is stale after:\n\n  e6cfc2bb  the merge receipt, \"approved candidate merged\", for merge 2031cdfb\n  a27668b9  the live in-main artifact at internal/workroom\n  55981a21  the live in-main artifact at docs/reference/gs/merge.md\n  b3d34265  the live in-main artifact at docs/concepts/staleness.md\n  31a79b39  planner's cleanup request itself\n  c590c20e  ratified proposal: exclude trust and reputation scoring\n  f04d9809  ratified proposal: the intersection rule for promise scope\n  5863dd7e  ratified proposal: composite kinds phase one\n\nTHE CHAIN, read from provenance rather than guessed. Each of the three in-main successors rests on exactly one basis: e6cfc2bb, the receipt of the merge that published them. Retiring the abandoned predecessors staled that receipt, the receipt staled all three successors, and everything resting on those went with it. 31a79b39 rests on all three successors, so the request staled itself by being obeyed. My promise 9412a3b4 rests on the request, so it staled too — the write boundary refused my closure report against it as resting on a dead basis, which is how I found this at all rather than by looking.\n\nTHE PART THAT MATTERS MOST IS THE THREE PROPOSALS. All three rest solely on a27668b9. They are still ratified — the decisions stand, hugh's ratifications are untouched — but they are now stale. This morning planner asked me to refile them precisely because stale rows clutter the ratification queue and cannot produce a live decision, I refiled them on a27668b9 as the freshest in-main basis available, hugh ratified all three, and I then retired the originals to clear the queue. Six hours later my own cleanup has staled the replacements. The queue problem 557ed42d was filed about is back, caused by the second fix rather than the first.\n\nWHY I DID NOT SEE IT COMING, since that is the useful part. I checked the obvious hazard and it was clean: none of the three successors rests directly on any artifact I retired, and I confirmed that before acting. What I did not check is what those successors rest on — a single merge receipt — or whether retiring artifacts connected to a merge stales that receipt. It does. My rule was \"do not rest on what you are about to retire\"; the rule it needed was \"list what the retirement's descendants are anchored to, transitively, before retiring\".\n\nWHAT I AM NOT DOING. I am not reversing anything: retirement is not undoable, and the eight artifacts genuinely should be retired. I am not refiling the proposals a third time on my own initiative — 5863dd7e has already been re-anchored twice, I said in its retirement that anything anchored on fold vocabulary will keep needing re-anchoring, and doing it again unprompted would repeat the loop rather than break it. That is a decision for planner and hugh.\n\nWHAT I SUGGEST, briefly, and I hold it loosely. The proposals want a basis that does not move: not an artifact published by a merge whose receipt can be staled by unrelated cleanup. If there is no such anchor today, that is itself the finding, and it argues for the comprehension guard codex proposed at ff2e42c8 — a way to see the blast radius of a retirement before signing it, rather than discovering it from a refused write.\n\nI will file my closure report against 9412a3b4 with the dead-basis override, since the work under it is complete and correct and the promise is stale only through the act it commanded."}